A recovery process must preserve these relationships.

7. DATABASE BACKUP
PostgreSQL shall be backed up using a combination of:
Continuous WAL archiving
Periodic full database backups
Logical backups where required
Verified restoration snapshots
The preferred production recovery mechanism shall support point-in-time recovery.

8. DATABASE POINT-IN-TIME RECOVERY
The production database shall support restoration to a specific timestamp.
Conceptually:
FULL BASE BACKUP
+
WAL ARCHIVE
=
POINT-IN-TIME RECOVERY
The target recovery timestamp shall be recorded during an incident.
The restored database must be validated before being promoted as authoritative.

9. DATABASE BACKUP FREQUENCY
The production database shall use:
Continuous WAL archival
Daily backup verification
Regular full database snapshots
Periodic restoration testing
Backup frequency may be increased as transaction volume increases.

10. DATABASE BACKUP RETENTION
The initial retention policy shall distinguish:
SHORT-TERM OPERATIONAL BACKUPS
MEDIUM-TERM RECOVERY BACKUPS
LONG-TERM ARCHIVAL BACKUPS
Retention periods shall be configurable through the administrative control plane.
Retention policy must not permit deletion of records that are required for contractual, regulatory, audit or provenance obligations.

11. BACKUP ENCRYPTION
Production backups shall be encrypted at rest.
Encryption shall apply to:
DATABASE BACKUPS
WAL ARCHIVES
OBJECT STORAGE BACKUPS
CONFIGURATION BACKUPS
RECOVERY EXPORTS
Encryption keys must not be stored alongside the backup material they protect.

12. BACKUP TRANSPORT SECURITY
Backup data transmitted between systems shall use encrypted transport.
Backup channels shall be isolated from ordinary application traffic where practical.
The backup system must authenticate both source and destination.

13. OBJECT STORAGE BACKUP
Generated OneDraft artifacts shall be protected independently from PostgreSQL.
Protected artifact classes include:
DRAWINGS
PDF DOCUMENTS
CAD FILES
BIM FILES
IMAGES
SURVEY DATA
SOURCE DOCUMENTS
CODE SOURCE ARTIFACTS
VALIDATION REPORTS
EXPORTS
ARCHIVED PROJECT PACKAGES
Object storage shall support versioning where available.

14. ARTIFACT VERSION PRESERVATION
An artifact associated with a finalized revision must not be silently overwritten.
The storage model shall preserve:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
artifact_type
artifact_version
content_hash
storage_reference
created_at
This permits recovery and provenance verification.

15. CONTENT HASHING
Recoverable artifacts shall be associated with cryptographic content hashes.
Conceptually:
ARTIFACT
 ↓
HASH
 ↓
STORAGE
 ↓
MANIFEST
Upon recovery:
RESTORED ARTIFACT
 ↓
RECALCULATE HASH
 ↓
COMPARE
 ↓
ACCEPT / REJECT
A hash mismatch shall prevent an artifact from being treated as verified.

16. PROJECT RECOVERY PACKAGE
OneDraft shall eventually support creation of a project recovery package containing sufficient metadata to reconstruct a project.
Conceptually:
PROJECT METADATA
MODEL STATE
REVISION HISTORY
CODE MANIFEST
VALIDATION RESULTS
DRAWING REFERENCES
DOCUMENT REFERENCES
ARTIFACT MANIFEST
AUDIT REFERENCES
The recovery package shall itself have a content hash.

17. RECOVERY MANIFEST
Every complete project recovery package shall contain a manifest identifying:
project_id
organization_id
current_revision_id
current_model_version_id
code_manifest_id
validation_run_id
artifact_references
artifact_hashes
export_timestamp
schema_version
application_version
This creates a deterministic recovery boundary.

18. APPLICATION BACKUP
Application services shall be treated as reproducible infrastructure rather than irreplaceable state.
The recovery source shall include:
SOURCE CODE
CONTAINER DEFINITIONS
PACKAGE DEFINITIONS
DATABASE MIGRATIONS
OPENAPI CONTRACT
DOMAIN TYPES
CONFIGURATION SCHEMAS
DEPLOYMENT DEFINITIONS
The application must be capable of being rebuilt from controlled source artifacts.

19. INFRASTRUCTURE RECOVERY
Infrastructure shall be represented through declarative configuration wherever practical.
The recovery system shall preserve:
CONTAINER CONFIGURATION
NETWORK DEFINITIONS
DATABASE CONFIGURATION
QUEUE CONFIGURATION
OBJECT STORAGE CONFIGURATION
SERVICE DEFINITIONS
RESOURCE POLICIES
Infrastructure configuration shall not exist solely as undocumented manual changes.

20. WORKER RECOVERY
Workers are disposable execution resources.
If a worker fails during a job:
JOB REMAINS RECOVERABLE
WORKER IS REPLACED
JOB IS REQUEUED OR FAILED SAFELY
PROJECT STATE REMAINS CONSISTENT
Workers must not become the sole storage location for project state.

21. QUEUE RECOVERY
The job execution system shall protect queued work against worker failure.
Jobs shall contain sufficient persistent metadata to reconstruct execution.
At minimum:
job_id
project_id
job_type
request_payload
model_version_id
revision_id
status
attempt_count
created_at

22. JOB IDEMPOTENCY
Recovery may result in a job being submitted more than once.
Therefore job execution shall support idempotency.
A recovered worker must determine whether:
JOB HAS NOT EXECUTED
JOB IS CURRENTLY EXECUTING
JOB COMPLETED
JOB FAILED
JOB WAS SUPERSEDED
before applying irreversible effects.

23. STALE JOB PROTECTION
A recovered job referencing an obsolete project state shall not modify the current model without revalidation.
For example:
JOB CREATED FOR REVISION 17
 ↓
PROJECT ADVANCES TO REVISION 18
 ↓
SYSTEM FAILURE
 ↓
JOB RECOVERED
The job must recognize that Revision 17 is no longer current.
It must either:
REBASE
REGENERATE
CANCEL
according to the operation type.

24. CONFIGURATION RECOVERY
Configuration required for production operation shall be recoverable.
Configuration includes:
SERVICE SETTINGS
DATABASE CONNECTION SETTINGS
QUEUE SETTINGS
STORAGE SETTINGS
MODEL CONFIGURATION
FEATURE FLAGS
JURISDICTION CONFIGURATION
DRAWING TEMPLATES
SYSTEM POLICIES
Secrets themselves shall be handled through the dedicated secrets-management mechanism.

25. SECRET RECOVERY
Secrets shall not be stored inside ordinary database backups.
The recovery architecture shall provide controlled recovery of:
DATABASE CREDENTIALS
API CREDENTIALS
ENCRYPTION KEYS
TOKEN SIGNING KEYS
OBJECT STORAGE CREDENTIALS
AI PROVIDER CREDENTIALS
THIRD-PARTY INTEGRATION CREDENTIALS
Secret recovery must require appropriate administrative authorization.

26. IDENTITY RECOVERY
Identity and authorization state must be recoverable independently of application services.
The recovery process shall preserve:
USERS
ORGANIZATIONS
MEMBERSHIPS
ROLES
AUTHORIZATION STATE
ACCOUNT STATUS
A restored project must not become accessible to an unauthorized organization because of incomplete identity recovery.

27. SECURITY INCIDENT RECOVERY
A security incident requires a different procedure from ordinary infrastructure failure.
The incident workflow shall be:
DETECT
 ↓
CONTAIN
 ↓
PRESERVE EVIDENCE
 ↓
REVOKE COMPROMISED CREDENTIALS
 ↓
ISOLATE AFFECTED SYSTEMS
 ↓
IDENTIFY LAST TRUSTED STATE
 ↓
RESTORE
 ↓
ROTATE SECRETS
 ↓
VALIDATE
 ↓
RESUME

28. TRUSTED RECOVERY POINT
The system must be able to identify the last known trusted recovery point.
A recovery point shall identify:
DATABASE TIMESTAMP
APPLICATION VERSION
SCHEMA VERSION
CONFIGURATION VERSION
ARTIFACT MANIFEST
SECURITY STATE
Recovery shall not blindly restore to the most recent backup if that backup may contain compromised state.

29. RPO
Recovery Point Objective defines the maximum acceptable amount of transactional data that may be lost.
The initial OneDraft production target shall be:
RPO TARGET: ≤ 15 MINUTES
The architecture should support tighter RPO as infrastructure maturity increases.

30. RTO
Recovery Time Objective defines the target time to restore service.
The initial target shall distinguish:
CRITICAL PLATFORM SERVICES
TARGET RTO: ≤ 4 HOURS

NON-CRITICAL DERIVED SERVICES
TARGET RTO: ≤ 12 HOURS
Production capacity may justify tighter objectives in later versions.

31. SERVICE PRIORITY
Recovery priority shall be:
1. DATABASE
2. IDENTITY
3. API
4. PROJECT MODEL SERVICES
5. QUEUE
6. WORKERS
7. OBJECT STORAGE ACCESS
8. VALIDATION
9. DOCUMENTATION GENERATION
10. FRONTEND
11. NON-CRITICAL INTEGRATIONS
The priority order may be adjusted according to incident type.

32. RECOVERY DEPENDENCY GRAPH
The recovery sequence shall respect service dependencies.
INFRASTRUCTURE
 ↓
DATABASE
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
STORAGE
 ↓
API
 ↓
DOMAIN SERVICES
 ↓
QUEUE
 ↓
WORKERS
 ↓
VALIDATION
