33. DATABASE RESTORE PROCEDURE
The database restore procedure shall conceptually execute:
STOP MUTATIONS
 ↓
IDENTIFY TARGET RECOVERY POINT
 ↓
RESTORE BASE BACKUP
 ↓
APPLY WAL
 ↓
VERIFY DATABASE INTEGRITY
 ↓
VERIFY SCHEMA VERSION
 ↓
VERIFY CRITICAL TABLES
 ↓
VERIFY PROJECT REFERENCES
 ↓
OPEN READ-ONLY VALIDATION
 ↓
PROMOTE

34. DATABASE INTEGRITY VALIDATION
After restoration the system shall verify:
PRIMARY KEY INTEGRITY
FOREIGN KEY INTEGRITY
UNIQUE CONSTRAINTS
REVISION CHAINS
MODEL VERSION CHAINS
PROJECT REFERENCES
AUDIT EVENT SEQUENCES
CODE MANIFEST REFERENCES
DOCUMENT PACKAGE REFERENCES

35. PROJECT STATE VALIDATION
Each recovered project must be checked for consistency.
At minimum:
CURRENT REVISION EXISTS
CURRENT MODEL VERSION EXISTS
MODEL VERSION BELONGS TO PROJECT
REVISION BELONGS TO PROJECT
CODE MANIFEST IS ACCESSIBLE
REFERENCED ARTIFACTS EXIST
ARTIFACT HASHES MATCH

36. REVISION CHAIN VALIDATION
The revision graph shall be traversable from the current revision back to the project's initial revision.
Conceptually:
REVISION 1
 ↓
REVISION 2
 ↓
REVISION 3
 ↓
...
 ↓
CURRENT REVISION
Broken parent relationships shall be treated as a recovery integrity failure.

37. MODEL VERSION VALIDATION
Model versions shall be verified against their stored state hashes.
A mismatch indicates that the restored model state does not correspond to its recorded version.
Such a state shall not be promoted without investigation.

38. ARTIFACT MANIFEST VALIDATION
The artifact manifest shall be compared against object storage.
The recovery system shall detect:
MISSING ARTIFACT
DUPLICATE ARTIFACT
HASH MISMATCH
UNEXPECTED ARTIFACT
BROKEN REFERENCE

39. DOCUMENT PACKAGE VALIDATION
A document package shall identify:
PROJECT
REVISION
MODEL VERSION
CODE MANIFEST
VALIDATION RUN
ARTIFACT HASH
Recovery shall verify that all referenced components remain available.

40. COMPLIANCE STATE RECOVERY
Compliance data shall be recovered as versioned project state.
The system must preserve:
CODE SOURCES
CODE RULES
CODE MANIFESTS
RETRIEVAL DATES
SOURCE HASHES
APPLICABILITY
VALIDATION RESULTS
A recovered project must not silently acquire a different regulatory basis.

41. VALIDATION STATE RECOVERY
Validation runs are historical records.
They shall not be silently regenerated and substituted for the original result.
The system shall preserve:
validation_run_id
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
code_manifest_id
results
timestamps
status
A regenerated validation may be recorded as a new validation run.

42. AUDIT EVENT RECOVERY
Audit events are append-only.
Recovery shall preserve their ordering and timestamps.
Recovered audit history must distinguish:
ORIGINAL EVENT
RECOVERY EVENT
RESTORATION EVENT
POST-RECOVERY EVENT
Recovery itself becomes an auditable event.

43. RECOVERY EVENT
The system shall create a recovery event containing:
recovery_id
incident_id
recovery_point
database_backup_reference
application_version
schema_version
operator_id
start_time
completion_time
result

44. BACKUP VERIFICATION
A backup shall not be considered reliable merely because it completed successfully.
The system shall verify:
BACKUP EXISTS
BACKUP IS READABLE
BACKUP HASH MATCHES
BACKUP METADATA IS VALID
RESTORE CAN BEGIN
Periodic complete restoration tests shall provide stronger assurance.

45. RESTORE TESTING
At defined intervals OneDraft shall execute restoration exercises.
The test shall:
SELECT BACKUP
 ↓
RESTORE TO ISOLATED ENVIRONMENT
 ↓
START SERVICES
 ↓
VERIFY DATABASE
 ↓
VERIFY PROJECTS
 ↓
VERIFY ARTIFACTS
 ↓
RUN INTEGRATION TESTS
 ↓
VERIFY DOCUMENT RECONSTRUCTION

46. RECOVERY DRILL
A full recovery drill shall test the ability to reconstruct the platform without relying on the original production instance.
The drill shall measure:
RECOVERY TIME
DATA LOSS
MISSING DEPENDENCIES
FAILED SERVICES
MANUAL STEPS
OPERATOR ERRORS
VALIDATION FAILURES

47. RECOVERY SCORE
Each recovery exercise shall produce a recovery assessment.
Conceptually:
RECOVERY SUCCESS
DATA INTEGRITY
SERVICE AVAILABILITY
ARTIFACT INTEGRITY
PROVENANCE INTEGRITY
SECURITY INTEGRITY
RPO PERFORMANCE
RTO PERFORMANCE

48. BUSINESS CONTINUITY MODES
OneDraft shall support operational states:
NORMAL
DEGRADED
READ_ONLY
RECOVERY
MAINTENANCE
EMERGENCY

49. READ-ONLY MODE
When transactional integrity cannot be guaranteed, the platform may enter:
READ_ONLY
In this mode:
PROJECT RETRIEVAL
DOCUMENT RETRIEVAL
HISTORICAL REVIEW
AUDIT REVIEW
may continue.
Mutating operations shall be disabled.

50. DEGRADED MODE
A degraded system may continue operating while non-critical services are unavailable.
For example:
DOCUMENT GENERATION OFFLINE
while:
PROJECT ACCESS
MODEL REVIEW
REVISION HISTORY
remain available.

51. EMERGENCY MODE
Emergency mode shall restrict the system to the minimum services required to protect project state and preserve operational continuity.
Potentially:
AUTHENTICATION
PROJECT READ
AUDIT
RECOVERY ADMINISTRATION
All other operations may be suspended.

52. REGIONAL FAILURE
The architecture shall eventually support recovery from loss of the primary infrastructure region.
Recovery resources should be capable of being provisioned independently.
The target architecture is:
PRIMARY REGION
       ↓
REPLICATED BACKUP
       ↓
SECONDARY RECOVERY REGION

53. STORAGE FAILURE
If primary object storage becomes unavailable, the system shall be able to restore artifacts from a secondary backup or archival source.
The project model must continue identifying the original artifact references and hashes.

54. DATABASE FAILURE
Database failure shall not cause loss of generated artifacts that are already stored independently.
Likewise, artifact storage failure shall not destroy the transactional project history.
This separation is fundamental to the recovery architecture.

55. CORRUPTION RECOVERY
Data corruption requires identification of the corruption boundary.
The system shall determine:
WHEN CORRUPTION BEGAN
WHICH PROJECTS ARE AFFECTED
WHICH REVISIONS ARE AFFECTED
WHICH ARTIFACTS ARE AFFECTED
WHICH BACKUP IS TRUSTED
Recovery shall use the latest trusted state rather than merely the latest timestamp.

56. PARTIAL PROJECT RECOVERY
OneDraft shall eventually support restoration of an individual project without restoring the entire platform.
The recovered project must preserve:
PROJECT MODEL
REVISION HISTORY
COMPLIANCE STATE
VALIDATION STATE
DRAWINGS
DOCUMENTS
AUDIT HISTORY

57. ORGANIZATION RECOVERY
The system shall eventually support organization-level recovery.
This includes:
ORGANIZATION
USERS
MEMBERSHIPS
PROJECTS
BILLING REFERENCES
ENTITLEMENTS
AUDIT EVENTS
Access controls must be reconstructed before project access is reopened.

58. RECOVERY ISOLATION
Recovered environments shall initially be isolated from production.
Conceptually:
BACKUP
 ↓
ISOLATED RECOVERY ENVIRONMENT
 ↓
VALIDATION
 ↓
SECURITY CHECK
 ↓
PROMOTION
No recovered environment should automatically become authoritative.

59. PROMOTION CRITERIA
A recovered environment may be promoted only after:
DATABASE VALIDATION PASSES
ARTIFACT VALIDATION PASSES
IDENTITY VALIDATION PASSES
APPLICATION HEALTH PASSES
SECURITY CHECK PASSES
INTEGRATION TEST PASSES

60. RECOVERY LOCK
During major recovery operations, mutating project operations shall be controlled through a recovery lock.
Conceptually:
RECOVERY_LOCK = TRUE
The API shall reject incompatible mutations until recovery validation is complete.

61. RECOVERY CONSISTENCY
The recovery process must preserve atomic relationships between:
MODEL
REVISION
GEOMETRY
VALIDATION
DRAWING
DOCUMENT
ACCEPTANCE
A recovered project must never expose a document generated from one revision while identifying another revision as its source.

62. VERSION COMPATIBILITY
Recovery must verify compatibility between:
DATABASE SCHEMA VERSION
APPLICATION VERSION
MODEL VERSION
ARTIFACT VERSION
OPENAPI VERSION
CONFIGURATION VERSION
Migration or compatibility procedures must execute before restored data is exposed to newer services.

63. DATABASE MIGRATION RECOVERY
Database migrations must be immutable and versioned.
The recovery process shall identify:
LAST APPLIED MIGRATION
TARGET MIGRATION
MIGRATION COMPATIBILITY
A migration must not be partially applied.

64. APPLICATION ROLLBACK
If a deployment causes operational failure, OneDraft shall support rollback to a previously known application version.
Rollback shall not automatically roll back database state unless an explicitly supported database migration reversal exists.

65. FORWARD-ONLY DATABASE MIGRATIONS
The preferred database strategy shall use forward-compatible migrations.
Conceptually:
VERSION N
 ↓
VERSION N+1
 ↓
VERSION N+2
Data preservation is preferred over destructive migration.

66. BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY
During deployment transitions, services may temporarily operate across adjacent application versions.
The API and database contracts should therefore support controlled compatibility during deployment.

67. RECOVERY CONFIGURATION VERSION
Every recovery point shall identify the configuration version active at that time.
This prevents the system from restoring data under incompatible configuration assumptions.

68. TEMPLATE RECOVERY
Documentation templates, title blocks and graphic standards shall be treated as versioned recoverable assets.
A drawing generated using a specific template version must remain associated with that version.

69. JURISDICTION CONFIGURATION RECOVERY
Jurisdiction-specific configuration shall be recoverable independently.
This includes:
JURISDICTION IDENTIFIERS
CODE SOURCES
RULE CONFIGURATION
UNIT SETTINGS
DOCUMENT STANDARDS
LOCAL REQUIREMENTS

70. AI CONFIGURATION RECOVERY
AI orchestration configuration shall preserve:
MODEL PROVIDER
MODEL IDENTIFIER
PROMPT VERSION
TOOL POLICY
COMMAND POLICY
CONTEXT POLICY
EVALUATION CONFIGURATION
The objective is reproducibility of AI-assisted operations where technically possible.

71. AI OPERATION PROVENANCE
AI-generated commands must remain associated with:
COMMAND_ID
PROJECT_ID
REVISION_ID
MODEL_VERSION_ID
AI_CONFIGURATION_VERSION
REQUEST
RESULT
VALIDATION
Recovery must preserve this provenance.

72. EXTERNAL INTEGRATION RECOVERY
External integrations shall be independently classified as:
CRITICAL
IMPORTANT
OPTIONAL
Failure of an optional integration must not prevent core project recovery.

73. EVENT BUS RECOVERY
Event-driven operations shall be recoverable through persistent event records where required.
Events that are necessary to reconstruct state must not exist solely in volatile message queues.

74. EVENT REPLAY
Where supported, the system may replay recoverable events.
Replay must be:
IDEMPOTENT
VERSION-AWARE
PROJECT-AWARE
AUDITABLE
Events must not be replayed against an incompatible model version.

75. DUPLICATE EVENT PROTECTION
Every recoverable event shall have a unique event identifier.
Consumers shall prevent duplicate application of state-changing events.

76. OBSERVABILITY RECOVERY
The observability system shall preserve sufficient operational telemetry to diagnose recovery events.
Important records include:
ERROR LOGS
SERVICE HEALTH
JOB HISTORY
DATABASE HEALTH
RECOVERY EVENTS
SECURITY EVENTS

77. RECOVERY MONITORING
During recovery, monitoring shall track:
DATABASE RESTORE
WAL REPLAY
STORAGE RECOVERY
SERVICE STARTUP
QUEUE RECOVERY
WORKER RECOVERY
PROJECT VALIDATION
ARTIFACT VALIDATION

78. RECOVERY ALERTING
Critical recovery failures shall generate operational alerts.
Examples:
BACKUP FAILURE
WAL ARCHIVE FAILURE
HASH MISMATCH
DATABASE CORRUPTION
RESTORE FAILURE
MISSING ARTIFACT
REVISION CHAIN FAILURE
SECURITY VALIDATION FAILURE

79. BACKUP FAILURE POLICY
A failed backup must not silently replace the last known valid backup.
The system shall retain the previous valid recovery point until a newer backup has been verified.

80. BACKUP STATUS
Backup records shall support states:
STARTED
COMPLETED
VERIFIED
FAILED
EXPIRED
RESTORED
CORRUPTED

81. BACKUP METADATA
Each backup should record:
backup_id
backup_type
source_system
created_at
completed_at
size
content_hash
schema_version
application_version
storage_reference
verification_status
retention_class

82. RECOVERY JOB MODEL
Recovery operations shall execute through the OneDraft job architecture where practical.
Recovery jobs shall contain:
recovery_job_id
recovery_id
operation
source_reference
target_reference
status
progress
started_at
completed_at

83. RECOVERY AUTHORIZATION
Recovery operations require elevated authorization.
The system shall distinguish:
NORMAL ADMINISTRATION
RECOVERY ADMINISTRATION
SECURITY ADMINISTRATION
No ordinary project-level permission shall permit platform-wide recovery.

84. RECOVERY AUDIT
Every recovery action shall generate an audit event.
The audit record shall identify:
WHO
WHAT
WHEN
WHY
FROM WHERE
TO WHERE
RESULT

85. RECOVERY DOCUMENTATION
The repository shall contain documented recovery procedures.
Initial structure:
/docs/recovery/
    database.md
    artifacts.md
    application.md
    infrastructure.md
    security.md
    full-platform.md

86. RECOVERY AUTOMATION
Manual recovery steps should be minimized.
The long-term objective is:
DETECT
 ↓
CLASSIFY
 ↓
PROVISION
 ↓
RESTORE
 ↓
VALIDATE
 ↓
PROMOTE
with human authorization at critical promotion boundaries.

87. AUTOMATED BACKUP VERIFICATION
The system should automatically perform:
BACKUP EXISTENCE CHECK
HASH CHECK
METADATA CHECK
RESTORE TEST
DATABASE HEALTH TEST
according to configured schedules.

88. AUTOMATED PROJECT RECONSTRUCTION TEST
At least one representative project should periodically be reconstructed from stored recovery material.
The system shall verify:
PROJECT LOADS
MODEL LOADS
REVISION CHAIN LOADS
GEOMETRY REFERENCES RESOLVE
CODE MANIFEST RESOLVES
VALIDATION RESULTS LOAD
DRAWINGS RESOLVE
DOCUMENT PACKAGE RESOLVES

89. DOCUMENT REGENERATION TEST
A recovered project should eventually be capable of regenerating its associated documentation.
The test is:
RECOVER PROJECT
 ↓
LOAD MODEL VERSION
 ↓
LOAD REVISION
 ↓
LOAD CODE MANIFEST
 ↓
RUN VALIDATION
 ↓
GENERATE DRAWINGS
 ↓
GENERATE DOCUMENT PACKAGE
The resulting artifacts should be compared against the recorded provenance state.

90. RECOVERY ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
A recovery is successful only when:
DATABASE IS CONSISTENT
PROJECTS ARE ACCESSIBLE
REVISION HISTORY IS INTACT
ARTIFACTS ARE AVAILABLE
HASHES MATCH
AUTHORIZATION IS CORRECT
SERVICES ARE HEALTHY
AUDIT EVENTS ARE PRESERVED
CORE INTEGRATION TESTS PASS

91. BUSINESS CONTINUITY TEST
The platform shall periodically demonstrate that it can continue operating after loss of a non-critical service.
For example:
DOCUMENT GENERATION FAILURE
must not prevent:
PROJECT ACCESS
MODEL REVIEW
REDLINE CREATION
REVISION MANAGEMENT

92. DEPENDENCY FAILURE TEST
The system shall test failure of:
DATABASE
QUEUE
WORKER
OBJECT STORAGE
AI PROVIDER
EXTERNAL INTEGRATION
where safe and appropriate.
The expected result must be defined for each failure.

93. AI PROVIDER OUTAGE
If the underlying AI provider becomes unavailable, the OneDraft project model shall remain operational.
The system must preserve:
PROJECT STATE
REVISION STATE
DOCUMENTATION
VALIDATION
AUDIT
AI-dependent operations may enter:
DEGRADED
QUEUED
UNAVAILABLE
without corrupting project state.

94. GEOMETRY ENGINE OUTAGE
If the geometry engine becomes unavailable, existing validated project state must remain readable.
New geometry operations may be queued until the engine becomes available.

95. DOCUMENT ENGINE OUTAGE
If document generation fails, previously generated document packages shall remain retrievable.
A failed generation must not overwrite a previously valid package.

96. COMPLIANCE ENGINE OUTAGE
Existing code manifests and validation results shall remain available.
